# os/fedora.capabilities — the Fedora capability declaration (Core v5, #663/#667).
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read (never sourced) by Core's zsh/02-capabilities.zsh into $_CORE_CAP, and by
# maint/dotfiles-maint.sh, which is bash. bootstrap.sh links it to
# $ZDOTDIR/os.capabilities via blib_link_os_layer.
#
# Transcribed from core/PORTING-MATRIX.md §"Package-manager commands", Fedora column.
# Validate with:  core/scripts/check-capabilities.sh os/fedora.capabilities
#
# THIS IS THE REPO core/examples/os.capabilities.example WAS WRITTEN FROM — the
# example's values are Fedora's, chosen because this repo is the template the other
# Linux repos stamp from. The example keeps the pedagogy; this file is the real
# declaration and carries only what is true of a Fedora box.
#
# A `#` INSIDE A VALUE IS NOT A COMMENT — the reader keeps it. Notes go above.

# ── package-manager verbs (all required) ──────────────────────────────────────
PKG_REFRESH=sudo dnf check-update
# THE INTERACTIVE upgrade verb — no `-y` here. `up` without a flag must still let dnf
# print its transaction summary and ask; auto-confirm is PKG_ASSUME_YES, below.
PKG_UPGRADE=sudo dnf upgrade --refresh
PKG_INSTALL=sudo dnf install -y
PKG_REMOVE=sudo dnf remove -y
PKG_SEARCH=dnf search
PKG_OWNS=dnf provides
PKG_COUNT_PENDING=dnf -q --refresh check-update

# ── scheduler (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SCHEDULER=systemd
SCHEDULER_UNIT_DIR=~/.config/systemd/user

# ── how `up` applies ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PKG_ASSUME_YES=-y
PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL=sudo dnf upgrade --refresh
# NO PKG_UPGRADE_PRE: `--refresh` makes dnf refresh and upgrade in one verb, so a
# separate pre-step would only re-fetch metadata dnf has already fetched.
# NO PKG_CLEANUP: dnf does not accumulate the way apt and brew do, and
# `dnf autoremove` on a workstation removes more than an unattended run should.

# ── how `up` counts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `dnf check-update` prints a header and blank lines before the package table; this
# ERE keeps only the rows that start with a package name. AN ERE CANNOT END IN A
# LITERAL SPACE — the reader trims trailing whitespace — hence [[:space:]].
PKG_PENDING_MATCH=^[a-zA-Z0-9][^ ]*[[:space:]]
# NO PKG_COUNT_EXIT_TRUSTED, and this is the archive that proves why it is off by
# default: `dnf check-update` exits 100 when updates EXIST. Trusting that status
# would report "could not answer" on precisely the boxes that have updates.

# ── the scheduled runner ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Fedora is a versioned, non-rolling distro whose stable updates are exactly what an
# unattended nightly is for, and this is what the box does today (Core's untethered
# maint arm ran `sudo -n dnf -y upgrade --refresh` here). The operator's
# MAINT_SYSTEM_UPGRADE=1 is still the FIRST of the two gates; this is only the second.
MAINT_UNATTENDED_UPGRADE=1

# ── optional tools ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NO TOOLS_OPTIN. PORTING-MATRIX.md's ²¹ table has no Fedora column to derive a delta
# from, and a declared list REPLACES Core's _CORE_DOCTOR_OPTIN rather than adding to
# it — so inventing one would mark every tool it omitted as expected-and-missing.
# Core's default is what this box reports today; inheriting it changes nothing.
